Coalesce feedback alias copies: a draw that samples an image whose only newer alias is one of its
own bound colour targets copies that target once per guest texture barrier and render target set,
not before every draw. Donkey Kong Bananza (Ingot Isle neon gate) binds one G-buffer target as a
2112x1179 colour target and samples the same memory as a 2095x1179 texture, so SynchronizeAliases
copied the whole target before every draw that sampled it (UpdateRenderTargets marks the target
modified at each draw, so the alias is always newer). The idea is from
heydemoura/EglantinePS5@5c3d5e9 (its feedback-copy part only); it is adapted to this core, not
copied: the alias walk cache, the overlap epoch and the vertex buffer scan are left out (99zu has
its own), and this core's render_targets_serial is only current once UpdateRenderTargets ran, which
is after the sampled views are prepared, so a draw coalesces only while Dirty::RenderTargets is
clear.
A skipped copy leaves the image's modification tick older than the target's, so it stays dirty: the
next barrier (fragment_barrier, invalidate_texture_data_cache, tiled_cache_barrier), render target
set change, compute read or read that is not a feedback read copies it. The remembered copy is
void, and the next sample copies again, when anything else wrote the image since (its tick moved:
another alias copied into it, a GPU write), when RefreshContents uploads the image (it forgets the
copy before any upload path), while the image is decoding or unswizzling in the background, and when
the source is deleted (its aliases forget it, so a reused id never matches). Not coalesced: compute,
a target that is unbound, format NONE or reached through another rt_control mapping, rescaled
images, and any sample with a second newer alias (those copies would overwrite what was
remembered), or a draw that changes the render target set. 99zw's shadow copies are untouched.
EDEN_NX2_FEEDBACK_COPIES=exact (or 0) copies before every draw; 0 also keeps 99zw's loop.
